  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying probable outcomes using a threshold (probability > 1/n) and computing their average. The individual does consider low-probability events (specifically the 1% outcome) and dismisses them, which aligns partially with the idea of filtering outcomes by probability. However, the individual does not mention the 33% outcome (2500) or explicitly evaluate which outcomes are 'probable' based on a systematic threshold. Moreover, the individual does not compute or compare averages of such outcomes. The reasoning focuses only on rejecting the 1% event as irrelevant, not on analyzing the average of the remaining ones. While there is a conceptual overlap in disregarding very low-probability events, the full procedure of the decision reason (threshold-based filtering and averaging) is not reflected. The justification is more heuristic ('won’t happen anyway') than structural. Therefore, it is unlikely the full reason was used, though a partial similarity exists in outcome filtering.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
